  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the domain of integration $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]\\times [0,\\pi /2]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061z\\,dz & =(4-1)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}\u2061z]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
